      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly identified the right side as the cathode. Now I can confirm that and point out that reduction occurs at the cathode, then match the reduction half‑reaction to the species present on that side. This lets me give the final answer while still showing the reasoning steps.",
      "content": "Yes, the right side is the cathode. At the cathode a reduction takes place, so we look for the species that gains electrons on that side. The half‑cell on the right is \\(\\text{Ag}^+(\\text{aq}) \\mid \\text{Ag}(\\text{s})\\). The reduction of silver ions is:\n\n$$\\text{Ag}^+(\\text{aq}) + \\text{e}^- \\to \\text{Ag}(\\text{s})$$\n\n**Final Answer:** A\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\text{Ag}^+(\\text{aq}) + \\text{e}^- \\to \\text{Ag}(\\text{s})$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\text{Ag}^+$: silver ions dissolved in the solution (the species that will gain electrons)\n  * $\\text{e}^-$: an electron, the particle that provides the reduction power\n  * $\\text{Ag}(\\text{s})$: solid silver metal formed on the electrode surface\n* **Why we use it:** In a galvanic cell the cathode is where reduction occurs. By looking at the cell notation, the species on the right side that can accept electrons is the silver ion. Writing its reduction half‑reaction shows exactly how the electrons are consumed to produce solid silver.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always remember that the cathode (positive electrode) is written on the right in cell notation, and the reaction there is a reduction. Selecting the half‑reaction that shows a gain of electrons for the species on that side will earn you the full mark for this question."
