      "content": "Correct – that’s the essential idea.\n\n**Final Answer:** Compounds with one or more double/triple bonds between C atoms in the hydrocarbon chain.\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n}$ (one double bond) or $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n-2}$ (one triple bond)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $n$: number of carbon atoms in the molecule\n  * $\\text{H}$: hydrogen atoms attached to the carbons\n* **Why we use it:** The general formulas show how the presence of a multiple bond reduces the number of hydrogen atoms compared with a fully saturated (alkane) formula $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+2}$. Recognising this pattern helps you identify unsaturated hydrocarbons quickly.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In DBE (National Senior Certificate) questions, always state that an unsaturated hydrocarbon contains *one or more* double or triple bonds. Forgetting the “or more” can cost you the second mark for the definition.\n"
