      "content": "Yes, the IUPAC name is **2,4-dimethylhexane**.\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\(\\text{Parent chain} = \\text{hexane},\\ \\text{substituents} = \\text{2,4-dimethyl}\\)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * **hex‑**: indicates a six‑carbon parent chain.\n  * **‑ane**: denotes that all bonds in the parent chain are single bonds (an alkane).\n  * **2,4‑**: the carbon numbers on the parent chain where the methyl groups are attached.\n  * **methyl**: a –CH₃ substituent.\n* **Why we use it:** IUPAC naming requires the longest continuous carbon chain as the base (hexane). Any side groups are named as substituents and numbered to give the lowest possible set of locants; here the methyl groups are on carbons 2 and 4, giving the smallest numbers.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always first locate the longest chain before naming substituents. Missing a longer chain (e.g., choosing pentane instead of hexane) costs you marks for the stem and can change the whole name.\n"
