      "content": "Correct, compound B is propan-1-ol. A positional isomer means we keep the same carbon skeleton (three carbons) but move the –OH group to a different carbon. Moving the –OH from carbon 1 to carbon 2 gives the IUPAC name **propan-2-ol**.\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\text{alkane\_root} + \\text{locant} + \\text{functional\_suffix}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * **alkane_root**: the base name for the number of carbons (\"prop\" for three carbons).\n  * **locant**: the carbon number where the principal functional group is attached (\"2\" because the –OH is on the second carbon).\n  * **functional_suffix**: \"‑ol\" indicating an alcohol.\n* **Why we use it:** The IUPAC system ensures every compound has a unique, descriptive name. By stating the locant, we tell exactly which carbon bears the –OH, distinguishing it from the 1‑ol isomer.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always number the chain to give the lowest possible locant to the principal functional group. For a three‑carbon alcohol, the two possible positions are 1‑ol and 2‑ol; the latter is the positional isomer.\n\n**Final Answer:** propan-2-ol"
