      "content": "2C8H18 + 25O2 → 16CO2 + 18H2O\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\[\\text{C}_x\\text{H}_y + \\left(\\frac{x + \\frac{y}{4}}{2}\\right)\\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow x\\text{CO}_2 + \\frac{y}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\]\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * **x:** number of carbon atoms in the fuel molecule (here, 8)\n  * **y:** number of hydrogen atoms in the fuel molecule (here, 18)\n  * **O₂:** di‑atomic oxygen from the air, the only inorganic reactant needed for complete combustion\n  * **CO₂:** carbon dioxide, the carbon‑containing product\n  * **H₂O:** water, the hydrogen‑containing product\n* **Why we use it:** Combustion converts every carbon atom to CO₂ and every pair of hydrogen atoms to H₂O. By counting C and H first, we can directly compute how many O₂ molecules are required (each O₂ provides two O atoms). Multiplying by 2 removes any fractional O₂, giving a clean integer‑balanced equation.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:**\n- **Formula mark (1):** Write the correct molecular formula of the fuel (C₈H₁₈).\n- **Substitution mark (1):** Insert the formula into the generic combustion template.\n- **Balancing/accuracy mark (1):** Ensure all coefficients are whole numbers and the equation is atom‑balanced.\n- **Units:** Not required for this question, but always write O₂, CO₂ and H₂O with correct subscripts.\n- **Common pitfall:** Forgetting to double the whole equation when O₂ comes out as a fraction; the examiner will deduct the balancing mark.\n\nYour final answer meets all three marks and avoids the typical error of leaving a fractional O₂ coefficient."
